From: j7
Subject: j7: hand the panel exactly what the client painted

The user reports purple where the desktop is black and 50 ms of row build in a 58 ms
present.  Both come from the same place.

The purple is this driver's own background: with no picture file on the volume, every
wide run of near-black pixels was replaced by a gradient that runs from 12/16/40 at the
top of the panel to 52/42/110 at the bottom.  That is a blue with a red lift - purple -
and it is painted exactly where the desktop draws its dark background.  Nothing needs
replacing there any more: the picture that gradient existed for was removed in 0392, and
the client already drew the pixels it wants on screen.  The gradient, the ramp and the
whole per-source-pixel pass that looked for those runs go away; only a picture file that
really exists still paints.

The other half is that pass.  0389 moved the picture decision to one per source pixel,
so the run-length expansion below it is now only a lookup and a store.  Keeping the old
scan meant a second walk over every output pixel looking for equal neighbours: 1334 rows
of 750 decisions plus 1080 run scans, on a CPU left at the bootloader's 908 MHz.  The
expansion is a flat loop over the output row now.

The shadow row is also only written when it is read.  It exists for the unchanged-row
skip, which is off whenever the double buffer is on, and fFlip is on: that skip can
never fire, so the 4 MB of cached copies per frame were dead stores.

Expected in the next log: `copy split build_us` down from 50 ms per frame, `fps` up from
7-9 in an interaction window, and no purple in the desktop's black - the panel shows the
client's own near-black.

Co-Authored-By: dipa-studio <183933817+dipa-studio@users.noreply.github.com>

